- `main.ts`: The entry point of the application where Vue is initialized.

Here's an example of a Vue component using the Composition API and TypeScript:

```vue
<!-- src/components/Products.vue -->
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>Products</h1>
    <ul>
      <li v-for="product in products" :key="product.id">
        {{ product.name }}
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</template>

<script lang="ts">
import { defineComponent, onMounted, ref } from 'vue';
import { useStore } from 'vuex';
import { Product } from '@/models/Product';

export default defineComponent({
  setup() {
    const store = useStore();
    const products = ref<Product[]>([]);

    onMounted(async () => {
      products.value = await store.dispatch('products/fetchProducts');
    });

    return {
      products,
    };
  },
});
</script>
```

In this example, the `Products.vue` component uses the Composition API with `setup()` function. It retrieves the list of products from the Vuex store using the `useStore` hook and displays them in the template. The `Product` interface is imported from the `models/` directory to provide type safety.

This is just a basic structure and example to get you started. You can further expand and customize the application based on your specific requirements, add more components, composables, and implement the necessary routing and state management logic.

Remember to use TypeScript throughout your application to benefit from type checking and improved tooling support.

